#: harwood/tasks/apextract.py:234
msgid "Find, trace and extract spectra from a 2-D frame."
msgstr "Achar, traçar e extrair espectros de um quadro 2-D."

#: harwood/tasks/apextract.py:27
msgid "A long-slit or fibre frame holds the spectrum running one way and the slit the other. Which axis is which is the first thing to get right: everything downstream is transposed if it is wrong, and the symptom is a trace that cannot be followed."
msgstr ""

#: harwood/tasks/apextract.py:27
msgid "Collapsing along the dispersion is what makes a faint spectrum findable -- a thousand rows of a few counts each become a peak you can centroid. It only works while the spectrum is straight enough that adding the whole frame does not smear it, which is what nsum limits."
msgstr ""

#: harwood/tasks/apextract.py:27
msgid "An arc has no star in it to find, and a faint exposure may not show its star well enough to trace. Both are extracted with the apertures of a frame that does -- the same centre, limits and trace -- so the arc's lines are measured on exactly the pixels the star's light was summed from."
msgstr ""

#: harwood/tasks/apextract.py:27
msgid "The reference's apertures, moved to where this frame's profile peaks: by default all by the median of their moves, so a small drift of the star on the slit is followed and the apertures keep their spacing."
msgstr ""

#: harwood/tasks/apextract.py:27
msgid "The strongest peaks in the spatial profile, none closer than minsep to a stronger one, each centred with center1d. One for a star on a long slit, many for fibres or an echelle."
msgstr ""

#: harwood/tasks/apextract.py:27
msgid "The finder is right most of the time, and the times it is not are the ones that matter: two stars it merged, a galaxy reaching past the threshold, a sky band landing on a neighbour. You have to look at the profile, so this shows it."
msgstr ""

#: harwood/tasks/apextract.py:27
msgid "The aperture is set where the profile, above a straight background between the minima either side, falls to a fraction of its peak -- a fibre and a long slit want very different apertures and the profile knows which it is looking at."
msgstr ""

#: harwood/tasks/apextract.py:27
msgid "The spectrum does not sit on one column. The camera has distortion and the spectrograph flexes as the telescope tracks, so the centre wanders by a pixel or several from one end of the dispersion to the other. Following it and fitting a low-order curve is what keeps the aperture on the light."
msgstr ""

#: harwood/tasks/apextract.py:27
msgid "Sky is measured in the sample regions either side of the aperture, fitted across the slit at every line, and the fit subtracted under the aperture. The gap between aperture and sample matters: the wings of the profile reach further than the aperture does, and sky measured on them includes the star."
msgstr ""

#: harwood/tasks/apextract.py:27
msgid "A plain sum of the pixels inside the aperture, the end pixels by the fraction of their light inside the edge. Every pixel counts the same, which is right when they all have similar signal and wrong when the edges are mostly noise. There is no sigma: IRAF only has one with variance weights."
msgstr ""

#: harwood/tasks/apextract.py:27
msgid "Optimal extraction (Horne 1986): the profile is fitted along the dispersion, each pixel weighted by how much of it it holds and divided by its variance, so the noisy edges stop diluting the bright middle; then the whole spectrum is scaled to the unweighted total, so only the noise changes. Cleaning replaces pixels the profile says are cosmic rays."
msgstr ""

#: harwood/tasks/apextract.py:27
msgid "One FITS file, one binary table per aperture, with the pixel, the flux, the uncertainty and the sky in it. Nothing has to be recomputed to read it, and `wspectext` turns it into text without knowing anything the file does not say."
msgstr ""

