#: harwood/tasks/ccdred.py:424
msgid "Bad columns and hot pixels carry no information, and a bad column running through a star ruins its photometry silently. Each bad region is replaced by interpolating across its narrowest side, as IRAF does -- a bad column along the rows, a bad row along the columns -- which makes the image look right without making the data real, so the mask travels with the frame."
msgstr ""

#: harwood/tasks/ccdred.py:424
msgid "The overscan is a strip of pixels the detector reads out without exposing them, so it carries the electronic offset the amplifier adds to every pixel and nothing else. That offset drifts during a readout, so it is fitted as a function of row rather than averaged to one number, and the fit is subtracted from the whole frame."
msgstr ""

#: harwood/tasks/ccdred.py:424
msgid "The edges of a CCD are not illuminated: the overscan strip, and usually a row or two at each border that the shutter or the mask clips. Keeping them means every later statistic is computed over pixels that never saw sky."
msgstr ""

#: harwood/tasks/ccdred.py:424
msgid "Even with the overscan removed, the bias has structure that varies from pixel to pixel and not from row to row -- fixed pattern in the amplifier and the clocking. A bias frame is a zero-second exposure, so it holds that pattern and nothing else; subtracting it removes what the overscan fit could not see."
msgstr ""

#: harwood/tasks/ccdred.py:424
msgid "Thermal electrons accumulate in each pixel whether or not light falls on it, at a rate that depends on the temperature and varies across the chip. The dark frame measures that rate, and is scaled to this exposure's time before subtraction -- twice the exposure, twice the dark."
msgstr ""

#: harwood/tasks/ccdred.py:424
msgid "Above its full well a pixel stops counting, and its value is a floor, not a measurement. It is flagged in the DQ while the frame is raw -- after the bias and the flat are off, the level it hit is no longer the number the detector reported."
msgstr ""

#: harwood/tasks/ccdred.py:424
msgid "With the overscan and the bias subtracted, what is left in a pixel counts the electrons it collected -- star, sky and dark current. Their number fluctuates as Poisson says, and the amplifier adds its read noise, so the variance is data/gain + (readnoise/gain)^2, in ADU^2. From here on it travels with the data: the dark and the flat add their own."
msgstr ""

#: harwood/tasks/ccdred.py:424
msgid "A flat is about to be divided into every frame of the night, so a pixel near zero in it -- a dead column, a vignetted corner -- would turn into an enormous value in each of them. IRAF raises anything below minreplace to it when it processes a flat, and so does harwood."
msgstr ""

#: harwood/tasks/ccdred.py:424
msgid "Pixels do not respond equally: quantum efficiency varies across the chip, dust on the window shadows it, and the optics vignette the corners. A flat field is a uniformly lit exposure, so it measures the response of every pixel relative to the rest. Dividing by it, normalised to one, turns counts into a quantity proportional to the light that arrived."
msgstr ""

#: harwood/tasks/ccdred.py:424
msgid "A dome flat is lit differently from the sky, by a pattern that is smooth across the chip. An illumination image holds that pattern, usually from dark-sky flats, and dividing by it, normalised by its mean, flattens the sky."
msgstr ""

#: harwood/tasks/ccdred.py:424
msgid "In the red, night-sky lines interfere in the thin chip and print a pattern that adds to the sky and does not divide. A fringe image holds it for some exposure, and it is subtracted scaled by the exposure times."
msgstr ""

#: harwood/tasks/ccdred.py:424
msgid "A zero's structure can run only along the readout, the same for every line. Averaging across that axis leaves the structure and removes the noise of all those pixels."
msgstr ""

#: harwood/tasks/ccdred.py:424
msgid "A scanned detector moves the charge while it exposes, so each pixel of the image was seen by several real pixels, and the calibrations have to be made the same way: a moving average over the scan, or a single line for a scan as long as the exposure."
msgstr ""

