msgstr ""

#: harwood/tasks/crutil.py:301
#, python-brace-format
msgid "{0} per cent of a frame was flagged. A dark exposure on a quiet site collects of order 0.01 per cent; a hundred times that is the detector's noise model being wrong, not the sky."
msgstr ""

#: harwood/tasks/crutil.py:301
#, python-brace-format
msgid "check --gain (now {0}) and --rdnoise (now {1}) against the header. Too low a gain makes every pixel look significant."
msgstr ""

#: harwood/tasks/crutil.py:31
msgid "The frame, and the gain and read noise that turn its counts into a noise estimate. Those two numbers are not cosmetic here: the whole method is a comparison against the expected noise, so a gain that is wrong by a factor of two moves every threshold."
msgstr ""

#: harwood/tasks/crutil.py:31
msgid "On a long-slit frame the object is a line, not a point, and the usual fine-structure model -- built to recognise a star -- sees nothing on the smooth flank of a spectrum. A second model, a median along the dispersion, estimates the spectrum without the cosmic rays in it, because a track is local along the dispersion and a median steps over it. Whichever model finds more real structure wins -- but only where the pixel agrees with the model, or a cosmic ray sitting on the spectrum would be protected along with it. Measured over six synthetic long-slit frames at the 1992 detector's gain: false detections on the flanks 114 without it and 0 with, for three tenths of a per cent of completeness."
msgstr ""

#: harwood/tasks/crutil.py:31
msgid "A cosmic ray deposits its energy in one pass, so it makes a mark sharper than the telescope can focus. L.A.Cosmic finds that by convolving with a Laplacian at twice the sampling -- the subsampling is what makes it respond to the edge rather than to the pixel grid -- and dividing by the local noise. A second image, the fine structure, models how much unresolved structure a real object would have there: a star is sharp but sits on a wing, a cosmic ray is sharp and alone."
msgstr ""

#: harwood/tasks/crutil.py:31
msgid "A track is bright in the middle and marginal at its ends, so pixels touching a detection are judged against a lower threshold. Whether the fine-structure test still applies while growing is the difference between a method that occasionally eats a star and one that does not."
msgstr ""

#: harwood/tasks/crutil.py:31
msgid "Each flagged pixel is replaced by the median of its *unflagged* neighbours. Unflagged matters: a track is several pixels, and a median that includes its neighbours swaps one bad value for another. Nothing is interpolated across a gap wider than the window, and the mask records what was touched."
msgstr ""

#: harwood/tasks/crutil.py:31
msgid "Several exposures of one field disagree only where something happened in one of them. That is ground truth for cosmic rays, and it lets the single-frame detector be measured rather than believed -- on these frames, with this instrument, at this threshold, rather than from the numbers in a paper. The frames are scaled to a common level first, because transparency changes between exposures and an unscaled comparison counts that real change as a cosmic ray in every bright pixel."
msgstr ""

#: harwood/tasks/crutil.py:31
msgid "The cleaned frame, and the mask beside it. The mask is the part worth keeping: it says which pixels are no longer measurements, so anything downstream can decline to use them rather than quietly averaging an interpolation."
msgstr ""

#: harwood/tasks/crutil.py:350
#, python-brace-format
msgid "the frames are not the same shape, so they cannot be compared pixel by pixel: {0}."
msgstr ""

#: harwood/tasks/crutil.py:350
msgid "trim them to a common section, or run imalign first."
msgstr ""

#: harwood/tasks/crutil.py:366
#, python-brace-format
msgid "the frames disagree far more on bright pixels than on faint ones ({0} per cent against {1}). A cosmic ray does not care how bright the pixel it lands on is, so this is the scene changing between exposures -- a source that varied, a profile that moved with the seeing, a trace that drifted. The completeness below is measuring that, not the detector."
msgstr ""

#: harwood/tasks/crutil.py:366
msgid "verify against frames of a static field -- darks, or a set where nothing moved. For these, judge the detections by looking at them, and reach for imcombine with rejection, which uses the same information without needing the scene to hold still."
msgstr ""

#: harwood/tasks/crutil.py:380
#, python-brace-format
msgid "the single-frame detector found {0} per cent of what the stack says is there. The rest are real cosmic rays it did not catch, and they are still in the cleaned frames."
msgstr ""

#: harwood/tasks/crutil.py:380
