msgstr "O combine do ccdred: imagens de um tipo, por subconjunto se pedido, com a escala do ccdred. Predefinição do imcombine."

#: harwood/tasks/imcombine.py:28
msgid "Combining needs frames of one shape taken of one thing. What the combination is *for* decides everything after: a bias measures the electronic pattern, a flat the pixel response, a stack of exposures the sky -- and each wants a different rejection."
msgstr ""

#: harwood/tasks/imcombine.py:28
msgid "Pixels outside these limits are dropped before anything else is measured. It is the blunt instrument: saturated pixels have no information left in them, and a saturated star in one frame would otherwise pull the rejection statistics around."
msgstr ""

#: harwood/tasks/imcombine.py:28
msgid "Frames taken through cloud, or with different exposure times, sit at different levels. Rejection compares each pixel against the others at that position, so the frames have to be brought to a common level first or the whole of a fainter frame looks deviant."
msgstr ""

#: harwood/tasks/imcombine.py:28
msgid "An additive offset, where the difference between frames is a pedestal rather than a scale -- a changing sky, typically, which adds the same counts everywhere rather than multiplying them."
msgstr ""

#: harwood/tasks/imcombine.py:28
msgid "Nothing is rejected: every pixel goes into the combination. Right when the frames are already clean, and wrong the moment a cosmic ray lands anywhere."
msgstr ""

#: harwood/tasks/imcombine.py:28
msgid "The lowest and highest values at each position are simply dropped, without asking whether they are deviant. Crude, predictable, and the right choice for a handful of bias frames where there is no noise model worth trusting and nothing to protect but the odd cosmic ray."
msgstr ""

#: harwood/tasks/imcombine.py:28
msgid "Each value is compared against the spread of the others at that pixel. That is its weakness as well as its strength: the threshold comes from the data, so with few frames two cosmic rays on the same pixel inflate the spread enough to hide each other, and neither is rejected."
msgstr ""

#: harwood/tasks/imcombine.py:28
msgid "The threshold comes from what the detector is known to do, not from the spread of the stack: sigma^2 = rdnoise^2/gain^2 + level/gain + (snoise*level)^2 -- read noise, photon noise, and flat-field error. Because it does not depend on the stack, a second deviant value cannot hide the first."
msgstr ""

#: harwood/tasks/imcombine.py:28
msgid "Like sigclip, but the spread is not measured at each pixel from a handful of values: it is pooled along the whole image line and scaled by the square root of the level, the way photon noise scales. With few frames that is a far steadier sigma than any single pixel can give, which is why IRAF's flatcombine uses it."
msgstr ""

#: harwood/tasks/imcombine.py:28
msgid "The spread is read off the stack itself: the value pclip places away from the median stands for one sigma. Robust when nothing is known about the noise."
msgstr ""

#: harwood/tasks/imcombine.py:28
msgid "A cosmic ray's bright core is rejected, and its fainter edge would not be; rejecting its neighbours within grow pixels, in the same image, takes the edge too. The combine is then redone without them."
msgstr ""

#: harwood/tasks/imcombine.py:28
msgid "Dithered frames do not start at the same pixel. Each goes where its offset puts it in an output large enough for all of them, and at each output pixel only the images that reach it are combined."
msgstr ""

#: harwood/tasks/imcombine.py:28
msgid "Each image's mask leaves out the pixels it marks before anything is measured or combined, the scaling statistics included."
msgstr ""

#: harwood/tasks/imcombine.py:28
msgid "The images are the planes of one cube, combined along its last axis."
msgstr ""

#: harwood/tasks/imcombine.py:28
msgid "Each image counts in the average in proportion to its level or its exposure -- IRAF's weighting, by the signal itself."
msgstr ""

#: harwood/tasks/imcombine.py:28
msgid "Each pixel of each image counts by the inverse of its variance, which gives the lowest noise any average of them can have: a pixel in a poor frame or under a bright star counts for less. It is not one of IRAF's, whose weights go by the image and by the level, whatever the noise is."
msgstr ""

#: harwood/tasks/imcombine.py:28
msgid "The median, except that of two values the lower is taken rather than their average."
msgstr ""

#: harwood/tasks/imcombine.py:28
msgid "The mean of what survived. Lowest noise when the survivors really are just noise, which is what the rejection was for."
msgstr ""

#: harwood/tasks/imcombine.py:28
msgid "The middle value of what survived. Noisier than the mean by about 25 per cent, and almost unmoved by anything the rejection missed -- which is why flats, where a few bad pixels matter more than a little extra noise, usually take the median."
msgstr ""

#: harwood/tasks/imcombine.py:28
msgid "Added rather than averaged, so the result is in the units of the total exposure. Only sensible when the frames are already on a common scale."
msgstr ""

#: harwood/tasks/imcombine.py:28
msgid "The square root of the sum of the squares, over n: how to combine images of errors or sigmas."
msgstr ""

#: harwood/tasks/imcombine.py:28
msgid "The noise the CCD model gives each value, from rdnoise, gain and snoise, combined in quadrature: an estimate of the sigma of the average, with no need of a sigma image from the data."
msgstr ""

#: harwood/tasks/imcombine.py:28
msgid "The combined frame carries IMCMB cards naming every input, so what went into it survives being copied somewhere else."
msgstr ""

