#: harwood/tasks/photometry.py:26
msgid "Pixels come in as floating point whatever the detector wrote. A measurement is never made on the integers a CCD happens to produce: a sum of sixteen-bit frames overflows silently, and a difference of unsigned ones wraps."
msgstr ""

#: harwood/tasks/photometry.py:26
msgid "The detection threshold is set in units of the noise, not in counts, so it means the same thing on a bright night and a dark one. The scatter is measured with clipping, so the stars themselves do not inflate the estimate of the noise they are to be detected against. The sky level itself is not needed: the detection kernel sums to zero."
msgstr ""

#: harwood/tasks/photometry.py:26
msgid "DAOFIND convolves the image with a Gaussian of the given width and looks for maxima in the result. The width is the whole parameter: a filter matched to the stars suppresses everything that is not star-shaped, so a cosmic ray, which is sharper, and a galaxy, which is broader, both respond less than a star of the same brightness."
msgstr ""

#: harwood/tasks/photometry.py:26
msgid "Sharpness and roundness are shape statistics, and they separate things a brightness cut cannot. A cosmic ray is too sharp to be a point source through this atmosphere; a galaxy is too broad; a bad column is too elongated."
msgstr ""

#: harwood/tasks/photometry.py:26
msgid "IRAF extends the image by its nearest pixel, so the filter is defined to the edge and a star half off the frame is still found. Its centre is less certain there, and phot loses its outer apertures, which is the reason to drop them."
msgstr ""

#: harwood/tasks/photometry.py:26
msgid "The sky is measured in an annulus around each star rather than once for the frame, because it is not flat: there are gradients, scattered light, and the wings of neighbours. The annulus starts outside the aperture so the star does not measure its own background."
msgstr ""

#: harwood/tasks/photometry.py:26
msgid "The aperture sum minus the sky over the same area. The radius trades light against noise: too small and a seeing change moves the answer, too large and the sky noise over the extra area swamps the star. Four or five times the FWHM catches essentially all the light."
msgstr ""

#: harwood/tasks/photometry.py:26
msgid "Instrumental magnitude is -2.5 log10(counts per second) plus a zero point, which is arbitrary until standards tie it to a real system. The uncertainty has three parts: photon noise from the star, sky noise over the aperture, and the uncertainty in the mean sky itself -- and in a crowded field the third one, which a naive implementation drops, is what dominates."
msgstr ""

#: harwood/tasks/photometry.py:26
msgid "The threshold is a judgement about this frame: too low and the noise is stars, too high and the faint ones are gone. Trying it on the image is quicker than reading a list, and f only looks, so that what is written is what was decided on."
msgstr ""

#: harwood/tasks/photometry.py:26
msgid "Coordinates leave here 1-based and (x, y), as IRAF and DS9 write them, with the convention recorded in the file so nothing downstream has to assume it."
msgstr ""

